1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,401
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,451 --> 00:00:02,852
Key Terms: Model Explainability.

3
00:00:02,902 --> 00:00:07,205
Thirty-one terms for judging an
explanation of a model, from the fluency

4
00:00:07,255 --> 00:00:10,106
trap and Shapley values to the audience
question.

5
00:00:10,156 --> 00:00:15,547
They matter because a faithful account of
the model can still mislead you about the

6
00:00:15,597 --> 00:00:15,910
world.

7
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:17,265
Hallo.

8
00:00:17,315 --> 00:00:18,793
This is Liam, in for Bear.

9
00:00:18,843 --> 00:00:21,850
A newcomer thinks a model's explanation
tells you why the outcome happened.

10
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:22,614
It does not.

11
00:00:22,664 --> 00:00:26,945
It tells you what the model used, and that
can be true about the model while it

12
00:00:26,995 --> 00:00:28,219
misleads you about the world.

13
00:00:28,269 --> 00:00:32,040
This film goes through thirty-one terms
for that gap, each one shown in a picture.

14
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:35,476
Five labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

15
00:00:35,526 --> 00:00:38,897
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

16
00:00:38,947 --> 00:00:42,032
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

17
00:00:42,082 --> 00:00:46,594
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

18
00:00:46,644 --> 00:00:49,444
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

19
00:00:49,494 --> 00:00:52,580
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

20
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:57,182
This table maps thirty-one terms in four
groups, all about telling an explanation

21
00:00:57,232 --> 00:01:00,160
of the model from a fact about the world.

22
00:01:00,210 --> 00:01:04,462
The first group is the fluency trap and
SHAP, short for Shapley additive

23
00:01:04,512 --> 00:01:04,793
explanations.

24
00:01:04,843 --> 00:01:08,102
Its terms are fluency trap, accurate but
misleading, shortcut, post-hoc

25
00:01:08,152 --> 00:01:11,080
explanation, SHAP, internal accounting,
Rung one and Rung two.

26
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:16,881
The second row is the Shapley value, what
the number is and the four guarantees

27
00:01:16,931 --> 00:01:17,615
behind it.

28
00:01:17,665 --> 00:01:21,649
The terms are value function, coalition,
marginal contribution and Shapley value,

29
00:01:21,699 --> 00:01:24,950
then the four axioms, Efficiency,
Symmetry, Dummy and Additivity.

30
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:31,270
The third row is computing it, and other
methods, how the values are estimated and

31
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:32,475
where they strain.

32
00:01:32,525 --> 00:01:36,890
The terms are sampling, as in KernelSHAP,
then TreeSHAP, correlated features,

33
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:40,100
conditional sampling, LIME, short for
local interpretable model-agnostic

34
00:01:40,150 --> 00:01:42,910
explanations, LIME instability, off-
manifold and counterfactual.

35
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:46,078
The last row asks, explainable to whom.

36
00:01:46,128 --> 00:01:48,946
Its terms are transparency,
explainability, interpretability, Rudin's

37
00:01:48,996 --> 00:01:52,224
argument, language game, language-game
mismatch and audience question.

38
00:01:52,274 --> 00:01:57,551
The first three are properties that
differ, and the exam expects the exact

39
00:01:57,601 --> 00:01:58,780
term for each.

40
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:02,912
A screening tool's report to a
radiologist, from a composite case, not a

41
00:02:02,962 --> 00:02:03,501
real incident.

42
00:02:03,551 --> 00:02:05,859
Score zero point eight four, two ticked
reasons.

43
00:02:05,909 --> 00:02:09,985
The fluency trap is taking smooth, well-
formed output as evidence, when nothing in

44
00:02:10,035 --> 00:02:11,754
it was checked against the world.

45
00:02:11,804 --> 00:02:16,764
For example, an agent tells a job seeker
she is a strong fit for a listing, in

46
00:02:16,814 --> 00:02:17,648
tidy, confident paragraphs.

47
00:02:17,698 --> 00:02:21,185
It reads so well that she applies without
checking the listing's actual

48
00:02:21,235 --> 00:02:21,480
requirements.

49
00:02:21,830 --> 00:02:22,296
Two boxes.

50
00:02:22,346 --> 00:02:24,874
About the model, features X and Y drove
the score.

51
00:02:24,924 --> 00:02:25,132
True.

52
00:02:25,182 --> 00:02:28,484
About the world, that X and Y mean
malignancy, the report says nothing.

53
00:02:28,534 --> 00:02:31,835
Accurate but misleading means true about
the model, yet the reader ends up

54
00:02:31,885 --> 00:02:33,382
believing something false about the world.

55
00:02:33,432 --> 00:02:36,734
For example, an agent explains that a
listing ranked first because it matched

56
00:02:36,784 --> 00:02:37,250
her keywords.

57
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:38,797
That is true of the agent.

58
00:02:38,847 --> 00:02:42,922
She takes it to mean the job is worth
applying for, but the role is already

59
00:02:42,972 --> 00:02:43,180
filled.

60
00:02:43,530 --> 00:02:47,251
Two grids of one hundred cases showing
feature X, with made-up numbers.

61
00:02:47,301 --> 00:02:50,079
In training ninety are malignant, in
deployment only thirty.

62
00:02:50,129 --> 00:02:54,792
A shortcut is a spurious pattern that
matches the label in training and fails on

63
00:02:54,842 --> 00:02:55,421
new data.

64
00:02:55,471 --> 00:02:57,935
An explanation naming X reports the
shortcut faithfully.

65
00:02:57,985 --> 00:03:02,334
For example, a model that predicts who
gets an interview is trained at one

66
00:03:02,384 --> 00:03:05,791
company, where referred candidates, who
mostly got interviews, used the company's

67
00:03:05,841 --> 00:03:06,733
own résumé template.

68
00:03:06,783 --> 00:03:07,990
It learns the template.

69
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:10,190
At other companies, the template predicts
nothing.

70
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:14,578
Input, model, output, and only then, down
the red dashed arrow, the explanation.

71
00:03:14,628 --> 00:03:18,980
A post-hoc explanation is a reason worked
out after the model has produced its

72
00:03:19,030 --> 00:03:19,294
output.

73
00:03:19,344 --> 00:03:23,382
It reports what the model did and adds
nothing the model never learned.

74
00:03:23,432 --> 00:03:26,841
For example, an applicant-tracking system
rejects a résumé, and afterwards a

75
00:03:26,891 --> 00:03:29,671
separate tool writes, rejected for missing
data analysis skills.

76
00:03:29,721 --> 00:03:33,130
That reason was built after the decision,
out of the decision.

77
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:35,477
Three bars from a made-up loan example.

78
00:03:35,527 --> 00:03:38,108
SHAP gives one number per feature, for one
prediction.

79
00:03:38,158 --> 00:03:42,494
The prediction is zero point seven two,
the average zero point five five, and SHAP

80
00:03:42,544 --> 00:03:45,418
divides that gap among income, debt-to-
income and zip code.

81
00:03:45,468 --> 00:03:48,927
For example, a fit-scoring agent rates one
listing for one job seeker.

82
00:03:48,977 --> 00:03:53,021
SHAP says how much the salary range, the
commute and the required skills each

83
00:03:53,071 --> 00:03:55,360
pushed that one score above the agent's
average.

84
00:03:55,710 --> 00:03:56,987
Left box, in the model.

85
00:03:57,037 --> 00:04:00,171
This is the internal accounting, how the
model itself divides credit among

86
00:04:00,221 --> 00:04:01,498
features, and it is real.

87
00:04:01,548 --> 00:04:05,479
Right box, in the world, questions SHAP
cannot touch, such as whether zip code is

88
00:04:05,529 --> 00:04:06,540
a proxy for race.

89
00:04:06,590 --> 00:04:10,520
For example, a model that predicts who
gets an interview may give much of its

90
00:04:10,570 --> 00:04:11,847
credit to the applicant's university.

91
00:04:11,897 --> 00:04:13,440
That is real about the model.

92
00:04:13,490 --> 00:04:17,155
Whether that university makes people
better at the job is a question about the

93
00:04:17,205 --> 00:04:17,420
world.

94
00:04:17,770 --> 00:04:18,291
Five questions.

95
00:04:18,341 --> 00:04:21,433
SHAP answers only the first, the additive
contribution to this prediction.

96
00:04:21,483 --> 00:04:25,717
That is Rung one, association, seeing what
goes with what, or what the model used.

97
00:04:25,767 --> 00:04:29,715
Cause, correctness and what happens if a
feature changes each get a red no.

98
00:04:29,765 --> 00:04:33,428
For example, a job-market report finds
that applicants who send a cover letter

99
00:04:33,478 --> 00:04:34,285
get more interviews.

100
00:04:34,335 --> 00:04:35,427
That is Rung one.

101
00:04:35,477 --> 00:04:39,140
It shows the two go together, not that the
letter caused the interviews.

102
00:04:39,490 --> 00:04:40,535
Two simulated scatter plots.

103
00:04:40,585 --> 00:04:44,913
Left, X and Y are only watched, and they
rise together because a hidden Z drives

104
00:04:44,963 --> 00:04:45,187
both.

105
00:04:45,237 --> 00:04:48,471
Right, X is set by hand, and the red line
goes flat.

106
00:04:48,521 --> 00:04:50,661
Rung two, intervention, is that level of
doing.

107
00:04:50,711 --> 00:04:51,755
SHAP cannot answer it.

108
00:04:51,805 --> 00:04:56,134
For example, a job seeker flips a coin for
each application to decide whether to send

109
00:04:56,184 --> 00:04:58,050
a cover letter, then compares interview
rates.

110
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:01,060
She set the letter by hand, so that is
Rung two.

111
00:05:01,410 --> 00:05:03,468
Three bars from a worked loan example.

112
00:05:03,518 --> 00:05:07,083
The value function is the expected
prediction when only some features are

113
00:05:07,133 --> 00:05:07,384
known.

114
00:05:07,434 --> 00:05:09,191
Nothing known, zero point five five.

115
00:05:09,241 --> 00:05:10,998
Income known, zero point six three.

116
00:05:11,048 --> 00:05:12,805
Everything known, zero point seven two.

117
00:05:12,855 --> 00:05:16,419
For example, ask an interview-prediction
model about one applicant when it knows

118
00:05:16,469 --> 00:05:20,033
only her years of experience, with
everything else filled in from typical

119
00:05:20,083 --> 00:05:20,636
past applicants.

120
00:05:20,686 --> 00:05:24,250
The average prediction it gives is the
value function for that set.

121
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:28,264
Eight boxes, every subset of three
features, income, debt-to-income and zip

122
00:05:28,314 --> 00:05:28,601
code.

123
00:05:28,651 --> 00:05:32,315
A coalition is a subset of features
treated as already known.

124
00:05:32,365 --> 00:05:37,380
The four red boxes are the coalitions
income can join, since they do not contain

125
00:05:37,430 --> 00:05:38,055
it yet.

126
00:05:38,105 --> 00:05:41,431
For example, a résumé-scoring model uses
skills, experience and education.

127
00:05:41,481 --> 00:05:44,807
Knowing an applicant's skills and
experience, with education still unknown,

128
00:05:44,857 --> 00:05:45,820
is one coalition.

129
00:05:46,170 --> 00:05:48,408
Four bars, one per coalition income can
join.

130
00:05:48,458 --> 00:05:52,127
The marginal contribution is what one
feature adds to the prediction when it

131
00:05:52,177 --> 00:05:52,985
joins a coalition.

132
00:05:53,035 --> 00:05:57,847
It depends on who is already in, here from
zero point zero six to zero point zero

133
00:05:57,897 --> 00:05:58,133
nine.

134
00:05:58,183 --> 00:06:02,138
For example, adding a portfolio link may
raise a hiring model's prediction a lot

135
00:06:02,188 --> 00:06:06,714
when it knows only the degree, and barely
at all when it already knows the work

136
00:06:06,764 --> 00:06:07,000
history.

137
00:06:07,350 --> 00:06:09,614
Six bars, one per order of arrival.

138
00:06:09,664 --> 00:06:13,910
The Shapley value is a feature's marginal
contribution averaged over every order of

139
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:14,241
arrival.

140
00:06:14,291 --> 00:06:18,537
The red dashed line marks it for income,
zero point zero seven three.

141
00:06:18,587 --> 00:06:21,842
It is average credit, not a prediction
from a subset.

142
00:06:21,892 --> 00:06:25,808
For example, a salary-estimate model's
credit for job title, averaged over every

143
00:06:25,858 --> 00:06:29,774
order in which title, location and
experience could arrive, is the title's

144
00:06:29,824 --> 00:06:30,435
Shapley value.

145
00:06:30,485 --> 00:06:33,410
The salary predicted from the title alone
is not.

146
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:34,641
A force plot.

147
00:06:34,691 --> 00:06:38,984
Three blocks push from the average, zero
point five five, to the prediction, zero

148
00:06:39,034 --> 00:06:39,915
point seven two.

149
00:06:39,965 --> 00:06:44,258
Efficiency, one of four axioms, means the
values add up exactly to the prediction

150
00:06:44,308 --> 00:06:46,120
minus the average, nothing left over.

151
00:06:46,170 --> 00:06:47,361
It is not correctness.

152
00:06:47,411 --> 00:06:48,602
No axiom promises that.

153
00:06:48,652 --> 00:06:52,324
For example, a salary tool puts one job
above its average estimate.

154
00:06:52,374 --> 00:06:56,047
The credit for title, location and
experience adds up to exactly that

155
00:06:56,097 --> 00:06:59,460
difference, whether or not they are why
the pay is higher.

156
00:06:59,810 --> 00:07:02,283
Two equal bars from a small invented game.

157
00:07:02,333 --> 00:07:06,383
Swapping the two features changes no
coalition's value, so each gets zero point

158
00:07:06,433 --> 00:07:07,013
zero five.

159
00:07:07,063 --> 00:07:10,798
Symmetry means features that do the same
work get the same credit.

160
00:07:10,848 --> 00:07:13,005
It says nothing about their causal roles.

161
00:07:13,055 --> 00:07:17,105
For example, if a résumé screener treats
the phrases customer service and client

162
00:07:17,155 --> 00:07:21,205
service the same way in every combination,
Symmetry gives the two phrases equal

163
00:07:21,255 --> 00:07:21,520
credit.

164
00:07:21,870 --> 00:07:24,548
The bottom row has no bar, just a red
zero.

165
00:07:24,598 --> 00:07:28,368
Feature D never changes the prediction, so
the Dummy axiom gives it zero credit.

166
00:07:28,418 --> 00:07:32,461
It follows that a non-zero value for zip
code means the model uses zip code.

167
00:07:32,511 --> 00:07:34,371
It does not mean the model should.

168
00:07:34,421 --> 00:07:38,463
For example, a résumé screener that never
looks at a résumé's file name gives the

169
00:07:38,513 --> 00:07:39,282
file name zero.

170
00:07:39,332 --> 00:07:42,829
If graduation year gets credit, the
screener uses it, and that says nothing

171
00:07:42,879 --> 00:07:43,920
about whether it should.

172
00:07:44,270 --> 00:07:46,679
Two trees, then the forest that adds them.

173
00:07:46,729 --> 00:07:50,060
Additivity, also called linearity, means
values for combined models are sums.

174
00:07:50,110 --> 00:07:54,671
Zero point zero seven plus zero point zero
two gives the forest's zero point zero

175
00:07:54,721 --> 00:07:57,744
nine, so a forest can be explained tree by
tree.

176
00:07:57,794 --> 00:08:02,047
For example, a job-match score is a skills
model's score plus a location model's

177
00:08:02,097 --> 00:08:02,355
score.

178
00:08:02,405 --> 00:08:06,965
The credit for commute distance in the
match score is its credit in each model,

179
00:08:07,015 --> 00:08:07,580
added together.

180
00:08:07,930 --> 00:08:10,044
A line chart from a simulated run.

181
00:08:10,094 --> 00:08:13,754
Sampling, as in KernelSHAP, estimates the
values from some random orderings instead

182
00:08:13,804 --> 00:08:14,991
of all of them.

183
00:08:15,041 --> 00:08:19,629
After forty draws the black line sits on
the exact value for income, zero point

184
00:08:19,679 --> 00:08:20,556
zero seven three.

185
00:08:20,606 --> 00:08:22,102
Accuracy is traded for time.

186
00:08:22,152 --> 00:08:26,121
For example, an agent explaining fit
scores for a whole day's job listings

187
00:08:26,171 --> 00:08:29,213
cannot try every order of its features on
each one.

188
00:08:29,263 --> 00:08:33,851
It samples some random orders per listing
and accepts a small error to finish in

189
00:08:33,901 --> 00:08:34,160
time.

190
00:08:34,510 --> 00:08:37,373
One decision tree, three splits deep,
eight red leaves.

191
00:08:37,423 --> 00:08:41,580
TreeSHAP gives exact values for tree
models by walking the tree's paths and

192
00:08:41,630 --> 00:08:44,492
weighting them, instead of trying every
subset of features.

193
00:08:44,542 --> 00:08:47,081
Thirty features would mean over a billion
coalitions.

194
00:08:47,131 --> 00:08:51,612
For example, if a job-listing ranker is a
forest of decision trees splitting on

195
00:08:51,662 --> 00:08:55,172
salary, location and required skills,
TreeSHAP gives exact credit for each

196
00:08:55,222 --> 00:08:56,790
listing without trying every subset.

197
00:08:57,140 --> 00:08:58,292
Two simulated scatter plots.

198
00:08:58,342 --> 00:09:02,198
Left, real cases for two strongly related
features sit on a pink band.

199
00:09:02,248 --> 00:09:07,306
Right, each feature is drawn on its own,
and half the draws, in red, land off the

200
00:09:07,356 --> 00:09:07,607
band.

201
00:09:07,657 --> 00:09:11,212
That is the correlated features problem,
combinations that never occur in real

202
00:09:11,262 --> 00:09:11,513
data.

203
00:09:11,563 --> 00:09:14,518
For example, job title and years of
experience move together.

204
00:09:14,568 --> 00:09:19,025
Drawn on their own, they produce a senior
director with no experience, or an intern

205
00:09:19,075 --> 00:09:20,527
with decades in the field.

206
00:09:20,577 --> 00:09:22,330
No real résumé looks like that.

207
00:09:22,680 --> 00:09:25,835
Now marginal draws on the left,
conditional on the right.

208
00:09:25,885 --> 00:09:29,681
Conditional sampling draws the missing
features given the known ones, so only

209
00:09:29,731 --> 00:09:32,565
four percent land off the band, against
fifty percent.

210
00:09:32,615 --> 00:09:37,372
But the result is no longer a classic
Shapley value, and the Dummy axiom can

211
00:09:37,422 --> 00:09:37,693
fail.

212
00:09:37,743 --> 00:09:41,218
For example, explaining a salary estimate,
conditional sampling fills in experience

213
00:09:41,268 --> 00:09:42,821
to match the known title.

214
00:09:42,871 --> 00:09:47,308
Then the number of past employers, which
the model ignores, can still get credit,

215
00:09:47,358 --> 00:09:48,910
because it travels with experience.

216
00:09:49,260 --> 00:09:52,945
A simulated curve of model output, with a
dashed line at one input.

217
00:09:52,995 --> 00:09:56,968
LIME perturbs that input, the black dots,
and fits a weighted line, in red.

218
00:09:57,018 --> 00:10:00,129
The slope is the explanation, here plus
zero point zero four.

219
00:10:00,179 --> 00:10:02,715
It is a local line, not SHAP's additive
credit.

220
00:10:02,765 --> 00:10:06,738
For example, to explain one job seeker's
interview score, LIME nudges her years of

221
00:10:06,788 --> 00:10:10,473
experience up and down, records the score
each time, and fits a line.

222
00:10:10,523 --> 00:10:11,910
Its slope is the explanation.

223
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:14,391
Same input, five random seeds, five lines.

224
00:10:14,441 --> 00:10:19,686
The slopes run from minus zero point three
one to plus zero point zero four, so even

225
00:10:19,736 --> 00:10:20,621
the sign changes.

226
00:10:20,671 --> 00:10:23,424
LIME instability means random
perturbations can give a different

227
00:10:23,474 --> 00:10:24,359
explanation each run.

228
00:10:24,409 --> 00:10:25,916
Most people run it once.

229
00:10:25,966 --> 00:10:29,966
For example, a recruiter runs LIME on one
rejected application, and keywords come

230
00:10:30,016 --> 00:10:30,900
out on top.

231
00:10:30,950 --> 00:10:34,950
Run again on the same application, a
different reason comes out on top.

232
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:37,582
Grey dots are real inputs, on an arc.

233
00:10:37,632 --> 00:10:41,664
Red rings are perturbed inputs that fell
off it, twenty-five of forty in this

234
00:10:41,714 --> 00:10:42,247
simulated run.

235
00:10:42,297 --> 00:10:44,871
Off-manifold means perturbed inputs that
no real case resembles.

236
00:10:44,921 --> 00:10:47,495
LIME returns a coefficient either way,
with no warning.

237
00:10:47,545 --> 00:10:50,993
For example, LIME explaining a cover-
letter scorer deletes random words from

238
00:10:51,043 --> 00:10:51,576
the letter.

239
00:10:51,626 --> 00:10:55,366
Many of the perturbed letters are broken
fragments no applicant would ever send,

240
00:10:55,416 --> 00:10:57,990
yet part of the line is fitted to them.

241
00:10:58,340 --> 00:11:00,304
A loan chart with an invented boundary.

242
00:11:00,354 --> 00:11:02,318
The black dot is a denied applicant.

243
00:11:02,368 --> 00:11:06,058
A counterfactual states how the prediction
would change if a feature had another

244
00:11:06,108 --> 00:11:09,511
value, here five thousand dollars more
income, or three points less debt-to-

245
00:11:09,561 --> 00:11:09,799
income.

246
00:11:09,849 --> 00:11:12,101
It is the model's answer, not the
lender's.

247
00:11:12,151 --> 00:11:15,841
For example, an agent tells a rejected
applicant the screener would have passed

248
00:11:15,891 --> 00:11:18,431
her résumé if it listed a project
management certificate.

249
00:11:18,481 --> 00:11:21,020
That is the screener's answer, not the
hiring manager's.

250
00:11:21,370 --> 00:11:25,025
A grid of open tiles, each a share of a
model's parameters.

251
00:11:25,075 --> 00:11:27,187
Transparency is a property of the system.

252
00:11:27,237 --> 00:11:29,966
Its code, weights, architecture and data
can be inspected.

253
00:11:30,016 --> 00:11:31,201
Open is not understood.

254
00:11:31,251 --> 00:11:34,906
A model can have one hundred seventy-five
billion parameters in plain sight.

255
00:11:34,956 --> 00:11:38,303
For example, an open-source résumé
screener publishes its code, weights and

256
00:11:38,353 --> 00:11:38,921
training data.

257
00:11:38,971 --> 00:11:39,847
It is transparent.

258
00:11:39,897 --> 00:11:44,170
A job seeker who opens the files still
cannot tell why she was rejected.

259
00:11:44,520 --> 00:11:48,241
Five applicants, each with a ranked row of
reasons, invented for illustration.

260
00:11:48,291 --> 00:11:50,127
Explainability is a property of outputs.

261
00:11:50,177 --> 00:11:52,327
Each output comes with a stated reason.

262
00:11:52,377 --> 00:11:54,841
The reasons may be more or less
informative.

263
00:11:54,891 --> 00:11:58,612
For example, an applicant-tracking system
attaches a reason to every rejection, such

264
00:11:58,662 --> 00:12:00,183
as, missing a required skill.

265
00:12:00,233 --> 00:12:04,897
Every output has a reason, so the system
is explainable, whether or not the reason

266
00:12:04,947 --> 00:12:05,840
helps the applicant.

267
00:12:06,190 --> 00:12:08,784
Top row, the model's output on ten new
cases.

268
00:12:08,834 --> 00:12:11,722
Below, an auditor's prediction for each,
with two red crosses.

269
00:12:11,772 --> 00:12:13,191
Interpretability is the person's side.

270
00:12:13,241 --> 00:12:17,892
A person can build a mental model good
enough to predict new cases, here eight of

271
00:12:17,942 --> 00:12:19,067
ten, an illustrative figure.

272
00:12:19,117 --> 00:12:23,474
For example, a career coach who has
watched a résumé screener at work can say,

273
00:12:23,524 --> 00:12:26,119
before a client applies, which new résumés
will pass.

274
00:12:26,169 --> 00:12:27,000
That is interpretability.

275
00:12:27,350 --> 00:12:31,104
The three properties, ticked for the
developer, crossed in red for the

276
00:12:31,154 --> 00:12:31,421
applicant.

277
00:12:31,471 --> 00:12:35,542
The contract asked for an explainable
reason, SHAP was shipped, and the affected

278
00:12:35,592 --> 00:12:37,444
person still cannot act on it.

279
00:12:37,494 --> 00:12:40,297
Rudin's argument is, for high-stakes
decisions, use interpretable models

280
00:12:40,347 --> 00:12:42,198
instead of explaining a black box.

281
00:12:42,248 --> 00:12:46,319
For example, by that argument, a résumé
screener that decides who gets considered

282
00:12:46,369 --> 00:12:51,074
for a job should be a simple scoring rule
recruiters and applicants can both follow,

283
00:12:51,124 --> 00:12:53,610
not a black box with SHAP added
afterwards.

284
00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:57,332
An agent's report says, the secret has
been deleted, above two lists.

285
00:12:57,382 --> 00:13:00,753
A language game is the setting that gives
a word its meaning.

286
00:13:00,803 --> 00:13:04,175
In the agent's game, deleted means its own
local state was wiped.

287
00:13:04,225 --> 00:13:07,596
In the user's game, it means no one can
reach the data.

288
00:13:07,646 --> 00:13:11,018
For example, to a résumé screener,
qualified means the résumé contains the

289
00:13:11,068 --> 00:13:11,588
listed keywords.

290
00:13:11,638 --> 00:13:15,010
To a hiring manager, qualified means the
person can do the job.

291
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:16,483
Ticks and crosses now.

292
00:13:16,533 --> 00:13:21,176
Three of three done in the agent's game,
none of three in the user's, as this

293
00:13:21,226 --> 00:13:22,349
course tells the case.

294
00:13:22,399 --> 00:13:25,868
A language-game mismatch is two parties
using one word for different operations,

295
00:13:25,918 --> 00:13:27,335
so a true report misleads.

296
00:13:27,385 --> 00:13:28,801
The agent did not lie.

297
00:13:28,851 --> 00:13:32,027
For example, a job agent reports,
application submitted, because the form

298
00:13:32,077 --> 00:13:32,614
went through.

299
00:13:32,664 --> 00:13:36,720
The job seeker hears that the employer has
her complete application, but the résumé

300
00:13:36,770 --> 00:13:37,600
upload had failed.

301
00:13:37,950 --> 00:13:40,602
Three questions, then a rewritten report
in the red box.

302
00:13:40,652 --> 00:13:44,386
The audience question asks who is reading
the explanation, what its words mean in

303
00:13:44,436 --> 00:13:46,548
their game, and whether it serves that
game.

304
00:13:46,598 --> 00:13:47,899
No attribution method catches this.

305
00:13:47,949 --> 00:13:49,250
It takes modeling the audience.

306
00:13:49,300 --> 00:13:53,034
For example, a fit score marked strong was
written for recruiters, where it means

307
00:13:53,084 --> 00:13:54,115
likely to pass screening.

308
00:13:54,165 --> 00:13:57,898
Before showing it to a job seeker, ask
what strong will mean to her.

309
00:13:57,948 --> 00:13:59,790
She may hear, likely to get hired.

310
00:14:00,140 --> 00:14:02,526
Two panels, a pair that gets mixed up.

311
00:14:02,576 --> 00:14:06,485
Left, Attribution, a number about the
model's accounting, such as income at plus

312
00:14:06,535 --> 00:14:09,225
zero point zero seven three in the loan
example.

313
00:14:09,275 --> 00:14:12,270
Right, Causal effect, an effect measured
by setting the feature.

314
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:15,620
It is a question mark here, because it
needs an intervention.

315
00:14:15,970 --> 00:14:19,367
Second pair, with numbers from an
exercise, not real data.

316
00:14:19,417 --> 00:14:23,504
Marginal SHAP draws features independently
and gives zip zero point one nine.

317
00:14:23,554 --> 00:14:28,329
Conditional SHAP draws features given the
known ones and gives zip zero point zero

318
00:14:28,379 --> 00:14:28,674
four.

319
00:14:28,724 --> 00:14:31,087
Both sets sum to zero point five.

320
00:14:31,137 --> 00:14:33,500
Neither is wrong, they answer different
questions.

321
00:14:33,850 --> 00:14:34,434
Third pair.

322
00:14:34,484 --> 00:14:38,556
Explainable means every output comes with
a reason, five of five squares here.

323
00:14:38,606 --> 00:14:42,995
Interpretable means a person predicts new
cases, eight of ten here, with two misses

324
00:14:43,045 --> 00:14:43,629
in red.

325
00:14:43,679 --> 00:14:47,751
A system can be fully explainable and
still not interpretable to the person

326
00:14:47,801 --> 00:14:48,068
affected.

327
00:14:48,118 --> 00:14:49,970
The exam expects the exact term.

328
00:14:50,320 --> 00:14:50,926
Last pair.

329
00:14:50,976 --> 00:14:53,880
The Model's counterfactual is what the
model would decide.

330
00:14:53,930 --> 00:14:58,146
At fifty thousand dollars the loan is
denied, at fifty-five thousand it is

331
00:14:58,196 --> 00:15:01,099
approved, and you check that by running
the model.

332
00:15:01,149 --> 00:15:05,037
The World's counterfactual is what would
actually happen, and that stays unknown

333
00:15:05,087 --> 00:15:06,350
until the lender decides.

334
00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:11,121
A vague request on the left, and on the
right what the exact version does.

335
00:15:11,171 --> 00:15:12,314
Not, explain this prediction.

336
00:15:12,364 --> 00:15:16,487
Say, list each feature's Shapley value,
and they must sum to the prediction minus

337
00:15:16,537 --> 00:15:19,468
the average, zero point one seven in the
loan example.

338
00:15:19,518 --> 00:15:20,660
The two bars match.

339
00:15:21,010 --> 00:15:22,858
Next, SHAP says zip code matters.

340
00:15:22,908 --> 00:15:27,285
The exact version, zip code moved the
model's output zero point zero three five

341
00:15:27,335 --> 00:15:30,131
above average, and that is not a causal
claim.

342
00:15:30,181 --> 00:15:33,610
The red segment is its share of the gap,
twenty-one percent.

343
00:15:33,960 --> 00:15:35,721
Next, LIME gave a different answer.

344
00:15:35,771 --> 00:15:39,948
The exact version, run LIME twenty times
with recorded seeds and report the spread

345
00:15:39,998 --> 00:15:41,760
of the slope, not one run.

346
00:15:41,810 --> 00:15:43,269
Each dot is one run.

347
00:15:43,319 --> 00:15:48,100
Here the slopes go from minus zero point
three three to minus zero point one two.

348
00:15:48,450 --> 00:15:49,973
Next, the system is explainable.

349
00:15:50,023 --> 00:15:52,805
The exact version names the property and
the audience.

350
00:15:52,855 --> 00:15:56,267
The dark bar is outputs with a reason, one
hundred percent.

351
00:15:56,317 --> 00:15:59,728
The red bar is new cases an auditor
predicts, eighty percent.

352
00:15:59,778 --> 00:16:02,560
The contract measures the first, safety
needs the second.

353
00:16:02,910 --> 00:16:05,367
Last, is zip a proxy for income.

354
00:16:05,417 --> 00:16:07,516
Report both versions side by side.

355
00:16:07,566 --> 00:16:12,173
In the exercise numbers, marginal sampling
gives zip zero point one nine, and

356
00:16:12,223 --> 00:16:14,680
conditional sampling gives zero point zero
four.

357
00:16:14,730 --> 00:16:18,620
Same total, a different split, and neither
settles the question alone.

358
00:16:18,970 --> 00:16:22,725
A checklist of six questions to ask before
you trust an explanation.

359
00:16:22,775 --> 00:16:27,163
First, does it describe the model, or does
it claim something about the world.

360
00:16:27,213 --> 00:16:29,382
An attribution describes the model's
internal accounting.

361
00:16:29,432 --> 00:16:32,870
A claim about the world needs evidence
checked in the world.

362
00:16:33,220 --> 00:16:35,148
Second, which rung is it on.

363
00:16:35,198 --> 00:16:38,445
SHAP and LIME stay on association, what
goes with what.

364
00:16:38,495 --> 00:16:42,402
A counterfactual reaches intervention,
what happens if a feature is set to

365
00:16:42,452 --> 00:16:44,710
another value, though only inside the
model.

366
00:16:45,060 --> 00:16:50,062
Third, do the values add up, and was the
sampling marginal or conditional.

367
00:16:50,112 --> 00:16:55,115
Efficiency says the Shapley values must
sum to the prediction minus the average.

368
00:16:55,165 --> 00:16:58,613
Marginal sampling ignores correlations
between features, and conditional sampling

369
00:16:58,663 --> 00:16:59,390
respects them.

370
00:16:59,740 --> 00:17:01,052
Fourth, is it stable.

371
00:17:01,102 --> 00:17:04,799
Run the same input again and see whether
the explanation holds.

372
00:17:04,849 --> 00:17:09,568
Then check that the perturbations stay on
the data, because an off-manifold line may

373
00:17:09,618 --> 00:17:10,590
describe nothing real.

374
00:17:10,940 --> 00:17:14,048
Fifth, which property was promised,
transparency, explainability or

375
00:17:14,098 --> 00:17:15,626
interpretability, and to whom.

376
00:17:15,676 --> 00:17:18,784
A reason attached to every output is
explainability.

377
00:17:18,834 --> 00:17:23,520
A person who can predict the system on new
cases is interpretability.

378
00:17:23,870 --> 00:17:28,529
Sixth, who is reading it, and what do its
words mean in their language game.

379
00:17:28,579 --> 00:17:29,785
Then say it exactly.

380
00:17:29,835 --> 00:17:34,180
Not, it explains itself, but, zip moved
the output zero point zero three five.

381
00:17:35,030 --> 00:17:35,619
Let's recap.

382
00:17:35,669 --> 00:17:38,815
A fluent, accurate explanation can still
mislead about the world.

383
00:17:38,865 --> 00:17:42,330
SHAP reports the model's internal
accounting and stays on Rung one.

384
00:17:42,380 --> 00:17:46,485
A Shapley value is a marginal contribution
averaged over every order of arrival.

385
00:17:46,535 --> 00:17:50,000
Efficiency makes the values add up, and no
axiom promises correctness.

386
00:17:50,050 --> 00:17:53,835
LIME can change between runs, and a
counterfactual is the model's answer.

387
00:17:53,885 --> 00:17:57,350
Explainable is not interpretable, so ask
who is reading the report.

388
00:17:57,700 --> 00:18:02,219
Give me short cases about explaining a
model's prediction, one at a time.

389
00:18:02,269 --> 00:18:07,140
For each case I must name the exact term,
chosen from fluency trap, shortcut,

390
00:18:07,190 --> 00:18:09,600
internal accounting, marginal
contribution, Shapley value, Efficiency,

391
00:18:09,650 --> 00:18:11,709
LIME instability, counterfactual,
interpretability and language-game

392
00:18:11,759 --> 00:18:14,521
mismatch, and say why its look-alike is
wrong.

393
00:18:14,571 --> 00:18:19,090
Hold back the answer until I reply, then
correct me in two sentences.

394
00:18:19,440 --> 00:18:21,370
Key Terms: Model Explainability.

395
00:18:21,420 --> 00:18:23,350
At Nik Bear Brown.
